1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,132
What if Earth stopped spinning...

2
00:00:02,152 --> 00:00:04,120
for just one second?

3
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:05,523
Sounds harmless, right?

4
00:00:05,543 --> 00:00:07,231
It's one second!

5
00:00:07,251 --> 00:00:08,035
Well...

6
00:00:08,055 --> 00:00:09,504
grab a snack.

7
00:00:09,524 --> 00:00:10,691
This is going to get weird.

8
00:00:13,691 --> 00:00:15,504
First, a confession.

9
00:00:15,524 --> 00:00:16,274
Right now,

10
00:00:16,294 --> 00:00:17,215
you are moving.

11
00:00:17,235 --> 00:00:18,339
Fast.

12
00:00:18,359 --> 00:00:20,129
Earth spins once a day,

13
00:00:20,149 --> 00:00:21,129
and at the equator,

14
00:00:21,149 --> 00:00:22,513
the ground beneath you travels

15
00:00:22,513 --> 00:00:24,904
about 1,670 kilometers per hour.

16
00:00:26,255 --> 00:00:27,178
The farther you are from

17
00:00:27,178 --> 00:00:28,045
the equator,

18
00:00:28,065 --> 00:00:29,069
the slower you go.

19
00:00:29,089 --> 00:00:32,727
New York cruises at about 1,270.

20
00:00:32,747 --> 00:00:35,388
London, about 1,040.

21
00:00:35,408 --> 00:00:36,658
At the North Pole,

22
00:00:36,678 --> 00:00:37,647
you're just turning in a

23
00:00:37,647 --> 00:00:38,386
very slow circle.

24
00:00:39,386 --> 00:00:40,724
And that's just the spin.

25
00:00:40,744 --> 00:00:42,088
Earth also races around the

26
00:00:42,088 --> 00:00:43,919
Sun at about 107,000

27
00:00:43,919 --> 00:00:45,274
kilometers per hour,

28
00:00:45,294 --> 00:00:46,459
while the Sun drags us

29
00:00:46,459 --> 00:00:48,617
around the galaxy even faster.

30
00:00:48,637 --> 00:00:51,322
Relax: nobody is stopping those.

31
00:00:51,342 --> 00:00:51,791
Today.

32
00:00:53,141 --> 00:00:54,424
So why don't you feel it?

33
00:00:54,444 --> 00:00:55,458
Because everything moves with

34
00:00:55,458 --> 00:00:56,654
you: the ground,

35
00:00:56,674 --> 00:00:57,777
the air, the oceans.

36
00:00:57,797 --> 00:00:59,119
It's like a smooth flight.

37
00:00:59,139 --> 00:01:00,134
You can sip coffee at

38
00:01:00,134 --> 00:01:02,611
900 km/h without spilling a drop.

39
00:01:03,961 --> 00:01:05,439
You already know this feeling.

40
00:01:05,459 --> 00:01:05,959
When a bus brakes hard,

41
00:01:05,979 --> 00:01:07,505
you lurch forward.

42
00:01:07,525 --> 00:01:08,545
That's inertia:

43
00:01:08,565 --> 00:01:09,749
moving things keep moving

44
00:01:09,749 --> 00:01:11,192
until something stops them.

45
00:01:11,212 --> 00:01:12,391
Now imagine the bus is

46
00:01:12,391 --> 00:01:13,532
the entire planet.

47
00:01:13,552 --> 00:01:15,518
And it's doing 1,670.

48
00:01:17,178 --> 00:01:18,601
Now, the experiment.

49
00:01:18,621 --> 00:01:19,391
Without warning,

50
00:01:19,411 --> 00:01:21,166
the planet slams on the brakes.

51
00:01:21,186 --> 00:01:22,529
Earth stops.

52
00:01:22,549 --> 00:01:23,593
You don't.

53
00:01:23,613 --> 00:01:25,096
Neither does the air.

54
00:01:25,116 --> 00:01:26,579
Or the oceans.

55
00:01:26,599 --> 00:01:29,091
Or that sandwich you were holding.

56
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:30,231
Half a heartbeat later,

57
00:01:30,231 --> 00:01:32,854
you're hurtling east at about Mach 1.4.

58
00:01:32,874 --> 00:01:34,023
That's faster than the speed

59
00:01:34,023 --> 00:01:34,710
of sound.

60
00:01:34,730 --> 00:01:35,730
In that one second,

61
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:38,440
you'd cover 465 meters:

62
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:39,485
about four and a half

63
00:01:39,485 --> 00:01:40,168
football pitches.

64
00:01:41,518 --> 00:01:42,498
The good news:

65
00:01:42,518 --> 00:01:43,679
you won't fly for long.

66
00:01:43,699 --> 00:01:44,691
Gravity pulls you to the

67
00:01:44,691 --> 00:01:46,303
ground in about half a second.

68
00:01:46,323 --> 00:01:47,403
The bad news:

69
00:01:47,423 --> 00:01:49,027
you land moving almost twice

70
00:01:49,027 --> 00:01:50,744
as fast as a cruising jet.

71
00:01:50,764 --> 00:01:52,122
Sideways.

72
00:01:52,142 --> 00:01:53,924
Into everything that just stopped.

73
00:01:53,944 --> 00:01:54,858
Which is...

74
00:01:54,878 --> 00:01:55,432
everything.

75
00:01:56,781 --> 00:01:58,328
Wait, would you fly off

76
00:01:58,328 --> 00:01:59,456
into space?

77
00:01:59,476 --> 00:02:00,425
Nope.

78
00:02:00,445 --> 00:02:01,668
To escape Earth's gravity

79
00:02:01,668 --> 00:02:03,186
you'd need about 40,000

80
00:02:03,186 --> 00:02:04,747
kilometers per hour.

81
00:02:04,767 --> 00:02:05,776
You're doing barely four

82
00:02:05,776 --> 00:02:06,713
percent of that.

83
00:02:06,733 --> 00:02:07,876
So you stay on Earth.

84
00:02:07,896 --> 00:02:08,809
Unfortunately.

85
00:02:10,159 --> 00:02:11,387
And it's not just you.

86
00:02:11,407 --> 00:02:12,154
Cars, trains,

87
00:02:12,154 --> 00:02:13,389
rocks, cows.

88
00:02:13,409 --> 00:02:14,749
Everything that isn't bolted

89
00:02:14,749 --> 00:02:17,272
down takes the same trip east.

90
00:02:17,292 --> 00:02:18,651
Then there's the air.

91
00:02:18,671 --> 00:02:20,026
The whole atmosphere keeps

92
00:02:20,026 --> 00:02:21,264
rushing east at up to

93
00:02:21,264 --> 00:02:24,309
1,670 kilometers per hour.

94
00:02:24,329 --> 00:02:25,610
That's more than three times

95
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:26,708
faster than the strongest

96
00:02:26,708 --> 00:02:28,311
tornado winds ever measured.

97
00:02:28,331 --> 00:02:29,592
Except this tornado is the

98
00:02:29,592 --> 00:02:30,496
size of a planet.

99
00:02:31,496 --> 00:02:33,123
The oceans keep going too.

100
00:02:33,143 --> 00:02:34,743
Water sloshes east and piles

101
00:02:34,743 --> 00:02:36,254
up against the western coasts

102
00:02:36,254 --> 00:02:37,733
of every continent,

103
00:02:37,753 --> 00:02:40,322
in walls of tsunami-sized waves.

104
00:02:40,342 --> 00:02:42,032
Somewhere, a surfer is having

105
00:02:42,032 --> 00:02:43,121
the best and worst day

106
00:02:43,121 --> 00:02:43,734
of their life.

107
00:02:45,134 --> 00:02:46,202
So who gets through this

108
00:02:46,202 --> 00:02:47,026
just fine?

109
00:02:47,046 --> 00:02:47,980
Astronauts on the

110
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:49,611
International Space Station.

111
00:02:49,631 --> 00:02:50,692
They orbit Earth instead of

112
00:02:50,692 --> 00:02:51,371
riding on it,

113
00:02:51,391 --> 00:02:51,951
so for them,

114
00:02:51,971 --> 00:02:53,677
it's just a very strange view.

115
00:02:53,697 --> 00:02:54,791
People at the poles barely

116
00:02:54,791 --> 00:02:55,603
move at all.

117
00:02:55,623 --> 00:02:57,313
Penguins are mostly fine too,

118
00:02:57,333 --> 00:02:58,353
though they don't actually

119
00:02:58,353 --> 00:02:59,940
live at the South Pole.

120
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:01,088
And pilots?

121
00:03:01,108 --> 00:03:02,421
Their planes already fly with

122
00:03:02,421 --> 00:03:03,543
the air around them.

123
00:03:03,563 --> 00:03:04,583
For one second,

124
00:03:04,603 --> 00:03:05,738
nothing changes up there.

125
00:03:06,738 --> 00:03:08,151
Now the twist.

126
00:03:08,171 --> 00:03:09,271
The second is over,

127
00:03:09,291 --> 00:03:10,422
and Earth starts spinning

128
00:03:10,422 --> 00:03:11,449
again, instantly.

129
00:03:11,469 --> 00:03:12,945
Anything still flying east is

130
00:03:12,945 --> 00:03:14,250
suddenly back in sync with

131
00:03:14,250 --> 00:03:15,027
the ground.

132
00:03:15,047 --> 00:03:16,076
Lucky.

133
00:03:16,096 --> 00:03:17,167
But everything that crashed

134
00:03:17,167 --> 00:03:18,796
and stopped during that second?

135
00:03:18,816 --> 00:03:20,195
The ground races back under

136
00:03:20,195 --> 00:03:21,306
it at full speed.

137
00:03:21,326 --> 00:03:23,377
So the whole disaster happens again...

138
00:03:23,397 --> 00:03:24,629
in the other direction.

139
00:03:25,979 --> 00:03:27,427
Physics would like a word.

140
00:03:27,447 --> 00:03:29,027
Earth's spin stores about

141
00:03:29,027 --> 00:03:32,457
2×10²⁹ joules of energy.

142
00:03:32,477 --> 00:03:33,808
Stopping it means dumping all

143
00:03:33,808 --> 00:03:34,742
of that somewhere.

144
00:03:34,762 --> 00:03:35,812
That's roughly what the Sun

145
00:03:35,812 --> 00:03:37,267
pours out in nine minutes.

146
00:03:37,287 --> 00:03:38,175
All of the Sun.

147
00:03:39,525 --> 00:03:41,586
And if Earth never started again?

148
00:03:41,606 --> 00:03:42,609
One day would last a

149
00:03:42,609 --> 00:03:43,303
whole year.

150
00:03:43,323 --> 00:03:44,803
Six months of daylight,

151
00:03:44,823 --> 00:03:46,197
then six months of night.

152
00:03:46,217 --> 00:03:47,163
And the Sun would rise

153
00:03:47,163 --> 00:03:47,958
in the west.

154
00:03:47,978 --> 00:03:48,838
Over time,

155
00:03:48,858 --> 00:03:49,837
the oceans would migrate

156
00:03:49,837 --> 00:03:50,918
toward the poles,

157
00:03:50,938 --> 00:03:52,263
leaving one giant continent

158
00:03:52,263 --> 00:03:54,311
wrapped around the equator.

159
00:03:54,331 --> 00:03:55,881
Here's the wild part:

160
00:03:55,901 --> 00:03:56,870
Earth's spin really does

161
00:03:56,870 --> 00:03:58,562
change, just a tiny bit.

162
00:03:58,582 --> 00:04:00,234
Tides, earthquakes and melting

163
00:04:00,234 --> 00:04:01,256
ice nudge the length of

164
00:04:01,256 --> 00:04:02,719
a day by milliseconds.

165
00:04:02,739 --> 00:04:03,989
In 2020,

166
00:04:04,009 --> 00:04:05,144
Earth even clocked some of

167
00:04:05,144 --> 00:04:07,035
its shortest days ever measured.

168
00:04:07,055 --> 00:04:08,335
To keep clocks honest,

169
00:04:08,355 --> 00:04:10,614
scientists have added 27 leap

170
00:04:10,614 --> 00:04:13,542
seconds since 1972.

171
00:04:13,562 --> 00:04:14,657
And they plan to stop

172
00:04:14,657 --> 00:04:16,212
by 2035,

173
00:04:16,232 --> 00:04:17,523
partly because leap seconds

174
00:04:17,523 --> 00:04:18,795
keep confusing computers.

175
00:04:20,455 --> 00:04:21,702
So, could Earth stop spinning

176
00:04:21,702 --> 00:04:22,746
for one second?

177
00:04:22,766 --> 00:04:23,725
No.

178
00:04:23,745 --> 00:04:24,648
Nothing we know of could

179
00:04:24,648 --> 00:04:26,010
brake a planet that fast.

180
00:04:26,030 --> 00:04:26,686
But if it did,

181
00:04:26,686 --> 00:04:27,760
the stop would be bad,

182
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:29,308
and the restart would be worse.

183
00:04:29,328 --> 00:04:30,498
On the bright side,

184
00:04:30,518 --> 00:04:31,637
you'd have the best excuse

185
00:04:31,637 --> 00:04:33,216
ever for being late.

186
00:04:33,236 --> 00:04:34,448
Hold on to your sandwich.

187
00:04:35,848 --> 00:04:37,134
That's one second you'll never

188
00:04:37,134 --> 00:04:37,829
get back.

189
00:04:37,849 --> 00:04:39,046
Spend the next five minutes

190
00:04:39,046 --> 00:04:40,139
on another what-if,

191
00:04:40,159 --> 00:04:40,938
right here.

192
00:04:40,958 --> 00:04:41,856
And if you want Bix

193
00:04:41,856 --> 00:04:44,152
to break more planets, subscribe.

194
00:04:44,172 --> 00:04:44,965
Responsibly.
